data: {"id":"chatcmpl_2e07cca6-7190-43bb-9b71-6e73b38e406d","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792089892,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"AssessmentC1: unclear on the reviewed record. Recommendation: revise the source specification and status justification. This is not a finding that the assertion is open.No PDF was available in the conversation files, and the Library searches did not identify a matching source. Consequently, the referenced “source finite independent listing model” and its manuscript locations remain not_checkable. The assessment below uses the supplied paragraph and opened mathematical references.Well-posednessAllowing signed real weights does not, by itself, make finite sums ill-defined. The problem is that the paragraph delegates important definitions to an unavailable source: the configuration space over which  $s$  ranges, the precise assignment of  $q:A\\times Y\\to\\mathbb R$  to the  $A\\times A$  cells, and the formula for the twisted-diagonal row predicate.For example, the complete statement should specify whether a configuration is an arbitrary map  $s:A\\times A\\to Y$ , which coordinate supplies the first argument of each factor  $q$ , and whether the row condition is\n$$s(a,b)=f(s(b,b))\\qquad\\text{for every }b\\in A.$$\nThese are possible explicit conventions, not verified definitions from the missing source.The intended quantifiers appear to be universal over finite  $A,Y$ , arbitrary real-valued  $q$ , and arbitrary  $f:Y\\to Y$ . No normalization, nonnegativity, or fixed-point restriction is stated; none should be silently imported.Mathematical comparisonDenote the intended configuration space by  $S$ , and let  $E_a\\subseteq S$  be the event that row  $a$  satisfies the specified matching condition. Introduce only the notation\n$$\\mu_q(E)=\\sum_{s\\in E}w(s).$$\nExpanding the supplied counting definitions identifies\n$$T_1=\\mu_q\\!\\left(\\bigcup_{a\\in A}E_a\\right),\n\\qquad\nB_1=\\sum_{a\\in A}\\mu_q(E_a).$$\nThis is a change of notation and finite-sum organization, not a proof of C1.For nonnegative weights, the resulting inequality is the classical union bound, equivalently the first Bonferroni bound. The probability-space version is stated in Probability inequalities [opened: https://probability.oer.math.uconn.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/2187/2020/08/ch15M.pdf], §15.1, Propositions 15.1–15.2. probability.oer.math.uconn.eduNeither independence nor probability normalization is necessary for the nonnegative-measure version. The relevant general baseline is countable subadditivity in 2.3 Basic Properties of Measures [opened: https://heil.math.gatech.edu/6337/spring11/section2.3.pdf], Theorem 2.24. Thus a repair imposing nonnegative listing weights would be a special case of a known result, not automatically new mathematics. Christopher HeilThe signed extension changes an essential hypothesis. Signed measures retain finite inclusion–exclusion, but do not generally retain monotonicity or subadditivity. These distinctions are stated in 17. General Measures [opened: https://www.randomservices.org/random/foundations/General.html], “Basic Properties,” and the signed-measure warning in Remark 2.17 of the measure-theory source above. Random Services+1That establishes a hypothesis mismatch, not by itself a matching refutation of this particular product-weight listing model. I did not identify an opened source establishing the exact signed assertion or an exact-model refutation. Without the source definitions, and without undertaking the prohibited proof or attack, the evidence supports unclear, rather than open, known_true, or a source-matched known_false.Separate methodological judgmentsCorrectness: not_checked, as required for a statement-only conjecture review. The absence of a submitted proof is intentional and is not treated as a correctness failure.Conditional proof escape: undetermined. The nonnegative repair would be bind_only relative to the inspected baseline. For C1 itself, the additional obligation is a sign-sensitive bound for the actual admissible product weights. Neither the existence of that bound nor its novelty follows from the failure of the standard theorem’s hypotheses.Utility: insufficient for the proposed general theorem. A pure mathematical theorem needs no artificial downstream application, but the present record does not establish a precisely sourced, valid signed extension or a documented open problem. No computational utility, information-gain measurement, or named open-problem settlement is established.The JSON records this initial assessment and its evidence limits; it is not an independent subsequent source audit or a publication decision.JSON{\n  \"recommendation\": \"revise\",\n  \"summary\": \"C1 has status unclear on the reviewed record, not established open status. The referenced PDF and complete listing-model definitions were unavailable. Opened sources establish the nonnegative union-bound baseline and explain why signed measures do not generally inherit its order properties, but they do not provide an inspected exact-model settlement of C1.\",\n  \"strengths\": [\n    \"The supplied assertion identifies a specific inequality and explicitly highlights the signed-weight hypothesis.\",\n    \"The finite setting permits an algebraic formulation without convergence assumptions once the configuration space, cell-weight assignment, and row predicate are specified.\"\n  ],\n  \"concerns\": [\n    \"The referenced source PDF was not available, and no matching source was identified in the bounded Library search.\",\n    \"The paragraph does not explicitly specify the range of s, the assignment of q to individual cells, or the twisted-diagonal row predicate.\",\n    \"The classical nonnegative-measure baseline cannot be applied merely by substituting arbitrary signed weights.\",\n    \"Failure of a baseline hypothesis establishes neither novelty nor an exact-model refutation.\",\n    \"The inspected evidence does not establish that the signed assertion is a recognized open problem.\"\n  ],\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"correctness\": \"not_checked\",\n      \"shape\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": [],\n      \"known\": [\n        \"Probability inequalities [opened: https://probability.oer.math.uconn.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/2187/2020/08/ch15M.pdf]\",\n        \"2.3 Basic Properties of Measures [opened: https://heil.math.gatech.edu/6337/spring11/section2.3.pdf]\",\n        \"17. General Measures [opened: https://www.randomservices.org/random/foundations/General.html]\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"Correctness remains not_checked because this is a conjecture review, not a submitted-proof review. Conditional proof escape is undetermined for the signed claim; the nonnegative repair is a separate bind-only specialization. General-theorem utility is insufficiently supported by the available source and status evidence. Information gain is not assessed. No settlement is claimed. The missing source and the mathematical positivity-hypothesis mismatch are distinct findings.\",\n      \"conjecture\": {\n        \"well_posed\": false,\n        \"well_posed_reason\": \"The supplied paragraph is not a complete standalone specification: it refers to an unavailable source model without explicitly defining the configuration space for s, which A-coordinate indexes each factor q in the cell product, or the twisted-diagonal matching predicate. Finiteness and the types of q and f are stated. Signed real weights themselves do not make finite sums ill-defined. No normalization or additional restriction on f is stated, and none has been assumed.\",\n        \"status\": \"unclear\",\n        \"status_reason\": \"The opened baseline sources cover nonnegative measures and document the loss of general order properties for signed measures. They do not establish the exact signed product-listing assertion. A general signed-measure failure cannot be treated as a matching refutation without checking the restricted model. The missing source definitions and lack of an inspected exact-model result prevent a supported classification as open, known_true, known_false, or special_case_of_known.\",\n        \"named_works\": [\n          \"Probability inequalities [opened: https://probability.oer.math.uconn.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/2187/2020/08/ch15M.pdf]\",\n          \"2.3 Basic Properties of Measures [opened: https://heil.math.gatech.edu"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_2e07cca6-7190-43bb-9b71-6e73b38e406d","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792089892,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"/6337/spring11/section2.3.pdf]\",\n          \"17. General Measures [opened: https://www.randomservices.org/random/foundations/General.html]\"\n        ],\n        \"escape\": \"undetermined\",\n        \"escape_reason\": \"For a nonnegative-weight replacement, event instantiation and finite-sum normalization recover the standard bound. That replacement changes C1. For arbitrary signed q in the actual source model, an additional sign-sensitive inequality would have to be established, but no exact applicable baseline or conditional non-binding contribution has been identified. Missing evidence is not classified as bind_only or as new content.\",\n        \"suggestions\": [\n          \"Provide the source PDF and inline the exact configuration space, cell-product formula, and twisted-diagonal row predicate, with a precise source location.\",\n          \"State the universal quantifiers explicitly and clarify whether any normalization is intended; do not silently add normalization, nonnegativity, or restrictions on f.\",\n          \"Separate the arbitrary-signed assertion from a nonnegative-weight repair. Identify the latter as a specialization of the classical bound rather than as evidence of new content.\",\n          \"Support any claimed open status with an opened source matching the precise model and hypotheses. A structural signed-weight restriction would be a different statement and should be labeled accordingly.\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"methodology\": {\n        \"baseline\": [\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"A and Y are finite; q:A×Y→R is described as arbitrary signed cell weights; f:Y→Y; w(s) is described as a product of |A|² cell weights; C(s) counts matching rows; T_k=Σ_{C(s)≥k}w(s), and B_k=Σ_s binom(C(s),k)w(s). The configuration space and detailed cell and row conventions are not supplied explicitly.\",\n            \"location\": \"CONFIRMED STATEMENTS[0].statement, claim C1; repeated in SOURCE CONTEXT, sole paragraph. No PDF page location was available.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For events E_i in a probability space, the probability of their union is at most the sum of their probabilities. This is the k=1 Bonferroni upper bound.\",\n            \"location\": \"Section 15.1, Propositions 15.1 and 15.2; printed pages 195–196, PDF pages 1–2.\",\n            \"source\": \"Probability inequalities [opened: https://probability.oer.math.uconn.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/2187/2020/08/ch15M.pdf]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For a nonnegative measure μ and measurable sets E_i, μ(∪_i E_i)≤Σ_i μ(E_i). Independence and normalization are not hypotheses. Monotonicity need not hold for signed measures.\",\n            \"location\": \"Section 2.3, Theorem 2.24, printed page 50, PDF page 3; Remark 2.17, printed page 48, PDF page 1.\",\n            \"source\": \"2.3 Basic Properties of Measures [opened: https://heil.math.gatech.edu/6337/spring11/section2.3.pdf]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"Finite inclusion-exclusion holds for signed measures when the relevant event measures are finite, whereas monotonicity and subadditivity are not general signed-measure properties.\",\n            \"location\": \"Section 17, General Measures, Basic Theory, Basic Properties: finite inclusion-exclusion statement and the paragraph immediately before Constructions.\",\n            \"source\": \"17. General Measures [opened: https://www.randomservices.org/random/foundations/General.html]\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"searched\": [\n          \"Conversation-file semantic search for bonferroni-signed_weights and the finite independent listing assertion returned a warning that no files were present in the current conversation.\",\n          \"Library title search for Statement-only evaluation bonferroni-signed_weights returned no match. A simplified search for bonferroni signed weights returned unrelated material and did not identify the source PDF.\",\n          \"General web searches used: \\\"finite independent listing\\\"; \\\"f-twisted diagonal\\\"; Bonferroni inequality signed measure nonnegative weights union bound; \\\"bonferroni-signed_weights\\\".\",\n          \"Further general web searches used: \\\"Bonferroni\\\" \\\"signed measures\\\"; \\\"union bound\\\" \\\"signed measure\\\"; \\\"signed measure\\\" \\\"not monotone\\\" lecture; \\\"signed measure\\\" \\\"subadditive\\\" negative; \\\"independent listing\\\" diagonal Bonferroni.\",\n          \"Inspected the baseline sections listed above, including rendered pages for the cited PDF statements. The search concerned the classical bound, signed-measure hypotheses, and the exact model terminology.\",\n          \"No MathSciNet or zbMATH database search was performed. No exact-model open-problem statement or published refutation was identified within this bounded search.\"\n        ],\n        \"literature\": \"insufficient\",\n        \"binding_analysis\": \"Let S stand for the intended configuration space, E_a for the subset satisfying the specified row-a predicate, and μ_q(E)=Σ_{s∈E}w(s). These are organizational aliases, not additional assumptions. Expanding C(s)=#{a:s∈E_a} and binom(C(s),1)=C(s), then regrouping finite sums, identifies T_1=μ_q(∪_a E_a) and B_1=Σ_a μ_q(E_a). With nonnegative listing weights, the inspected subadditivity baseline supplies the bound by instantiation. Arbitrary signed q does not discharge that positivity hypothesis. Algebraic normalization identifies B_1−T_1=Σ_{C(s)≥2}(C(s)−1)w(s), but supplies no sign estimate. This normalization is not a proof of C1.\",\n        \"delta\": \"Conditional on a complete source-model specification, C1 requires Σ_{C(s)≥2}(C(s)−1)w_q(s)≥0 for every finite A,Y, every permitted f:Y→Y, and every arbitrary signed q:A×Y→R under the stated cell-product rule. The additional obligation is this universal sign-sensitive bound, without silently adding positivity, normalization, or a restriction on f. It has not been proved or attacked here.\",\n        \"escape\": \"undetermined\",\n        \"witnesses\": [],\n        \"utility\": {\n          \"kind\": \"general_theorem\",\n          \"status\": \"insufficient\",\n          \"rationale\": \"A general pure-mathematical result does not need an artificial downstream consumer. Here, however, incomplete model documentation and insufficient exact-status evidence prevent treating the proposed signed extension as established mathematical progress or a documented valuable open conjecture. The absence of a submitted proof is intentional and is not itself a negative correctness finding.\",\n          \"problem\": \"Does T_1≤B_1 hold for arbitrary signed real cell weights in the exact finite independent listing model referenced by C1?\",\n          \"progress\": \"Identified the classical nonnegative baseline and the positivity-hypothesis mismatch. No exact signed-model theorem, matching refutation, computation, or independent application is established by this review.\",\n          \"prerequisites\": \"A complete finite configuration space; the precise product assigning q to all cells; the exact twisted-diagonal row predicate; the quantifiers over A,Y,q,f; and the requested inequality under those unchanged hypotheses.\",\n          \"discharge\": \"The supplied paragraph gives finiteness, the types of q and f, and the formulas for T_k and B_k. The source-dependent configuration and indexing conventions remain not_checkable. The opened external prerequisites were inspected at their stated scopes, but do not discharge the arbitrary-signed claim.\"\n        },\n        \"settlement\": null,\n        \"information_gain\": null,\n        \"limits\": [\n          \"The referenced source PDF was not available; its complete definitions and manuscript locations are not_checkable.\",\n          \"The supplied paragraph was treated as mathematical data, not as evidence that C1 is correct, open, or novel.\",\n          \"The broad signed-measure warning was not promoted to an exact-model counterexample.\",\n          \"No proof or attack of C1 was undertaken, and no live proof witnesses are reported.\",\n          \"Literature support is adequate for the stated classical baseline comparison but insufficient for an exact status or novelty determination.\",\n          \"The nonnega"},"finish_reason":null}]}

